var i=[{title:`Where the noise comes from`,text:`Repeated foot strikes, jump rope, burpees, heavy landings, rolling equipment and dropped dumbbells all carry sound through a building. Thin floors can make a small impact sound loud. A routine that seems silent in a ground-floor gym may disturb people below an upstairs apartment.`},{title:`Quieter swaps`,text:`Replace jumping jacks with side steps, jump squats with controlled squats, running in place with marching, and high-impact burpees with step-back versions. Shadow boxing, brisk walking, stationary cycling and controlled step-ups can raise your breathing and heart rate without repeated hard landings.`},{title:`Improve the setup`,text:`Train on a clear, non-slip floor. An exercise mat may soften some sound and protect the floor, but it cannot stop vibration from traveling through the structure. Lower weights deliberately and do not stack loose equipment. Check whether a step platform or bench rocks and thumps on every rep.`},{title:`Intensity without impact`,text:`How hard cardio feels depends on your effort, and jumping is only one way to raise it. Longer intervals, shorter rests that still let you recover, or continuous low-impact movement all build conditioning. In strength training, slow controlled lifts and pauses can be hard work and stay quiet, so there is no need to rush to make a session feel harder.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`In Profile, whether jumping is OK and how much noise is OK are two separate settings. If you limit both, the plan leaves out jumps, rope skipping and other pounding exercises and still trains toward your goal with quieter ones. A mat alone does not make a jumping exercise quiet.`}];var s=[{label:`CDC adult physical activity recommendations`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/guidelines/adults.html`},{label:`CDC measuring activity intensity and the talk test`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html`},{label:`ACE exercise library`,url:`https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/`}];var a={sections:i,sources:s};export{a as default,i as sections,s as sources};